WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.200
Every field has a float rod.

00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.550
The red rings above the sleeve tell you the water level.

00:00:05.950 --> 00:00:09.900
The goal: read seven in ten photos exactly, and be sure of it.

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.275
We tried three ways.

00:00:12.675 --> 00:00:16.800
The company's detector, version zero, hunts for the pinkest column.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:23.525
Our friend's method isolates the colour, remembers where each gauge stands, and carries it across the two shots.

00:00:23.935 --> 00:00:27.375
Our reader measures the ring rhythm, so hidden rings still count.

00:00:28.360 --> 00:00:32.200
On the same hundred and twenty seven photos, none was good enough.

00:00:32.600 --> 00:00:37.575
Our reader was right seventy eight percent of the time, but it was rarely sure enough to say so.

00:00:38.600 --> 00:00:41.300
Version one takes the best idea from each.

00:00:41.690 --> 00:00:48.675
It finds the rod by colour, reads the ring rhythm, checks the visit's other shot, and reads a bare grey rod as dry.

00:00:49.105 --> 00:00:52.300
Then a trained model gives the chance the count is exact.

00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:55.050
The company's rule decides.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.975
Accept at seventy percent, average the accepted shots, and turn the count into a soil state.

00:01:01.385 --> 00:01:05.275
A reading that breaks the gauge's physics is flagged as a bad gauge.

00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:11.225
Result: ninety two of a hundred and twenty seven photos, accepted and exact.

00:01:11.625 --> 00:01:14.200
Seventy two percent, inside the target.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:20.875
Here, version zero reads ten, our friend's method twenty three, and version one twenty four, which is right.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:25.475
Next, a fresh batch of field photos is the real test.

00:01:25.925 --> 00:01:28.950
Version one: read every ring, and know when you're sure.
